## PR Reviewer Guide 🔍

<!-- pr-agent:review:full -->

Here are some key observations to aid the review process:

<table>
<tr><td>

**🎫 Ticket compliance analysis ✅**



**[114](https://github.com/fixture-owner/snaptally/issues/114) - PR Code Verified**

Compliant requirements:

- Replaced `<datalist>` with Alpine.js dropdown.
- Queries `allMerchants` local array.
- Case-insensitive substring/prefix filtering.
- Top 5 suggestions with prefix/recency/frequency ranking.
- Tap selection populates input and closes dropdown.
- Keyboard navigation for ArrowDown/ArrowUp/Enter/Escape.
- Click-outside dismissal.
- Unit tests for filtering, ranking, keyboard navigation.

Requires further human verification:

- Mobile browser ergonomics (iOS Safari / WebKit) and touch-friendly dropdown behavior in a real device.
- Visual/responsive rendering of the floating dropdown and ARIA combobox behavior with screen readers.
- Whether `allMerchants` is actually synced from `GET /api/merchants` (the diff only shows local cache usage; sync path not visible).



</td></tr>
<tr><td>⏱️&nbsp;<strong>Estimated effort to review</strong>: 3 🔵🔵🔵⚪⚪</td></tr>
<tr><td>🧪&nbsp;<strong>PR contains tests</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>🔒&nbsp;<strong>No security concerns identified</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>⚡&nbsp;<strong>Recommended focus areas for review</strong><br><br>

<details><summary><a href='https://github.com/fixture-owner/snaptally/pull/121/files#diff-42b67a3f58f6e0b4532f78652ecc5c7f6c9800f6ce1721f2b92cff4b51301f6aR197-R262'><strong>Architectural Verdict</strong></a>

ARCHITECTURALLY SOUND BUT OVERBUILT. Underlying use case (mobile users need touch-friendly merchant suggestions) is real and demonstrated by the ticket's iOS Safari rationale. However, the implementation adds ~150 lines of custom combobox logic (ranking, keyboard state machine, ARIA wiring, focus/outside-click handling) to replace a native `<datalist>` that already existed. The strongest simpler alternative: keep the `<datalist>` as the baseline and only add a minimal filtered list if a concrete mobile failure is reproduced; or extract the ranking/keyboard logic into a small reusable helper rather than embedding it in the already-large `intakeForm` object. The ranking rules (prefix > recency > frequency > alphabetical) are a speculative product decision not required by the ticket, which only asks for "recency/frequency" ranking. No ADR was added for replacing the native control, which is a user-facing, hard-to-reverse UX decision that will be questioned later. Recommend an ADR capturing context (datalist mobile limitations), alternatives (datalist, native `<select>`, third-party combobox), decision, and revisit conditions. Final Challenge: if half the new architecture had to be removed, remove the custom ranking tiers (keep simple substring filter + alphabetical) and the `onCustomMerchantFocus` reopen logic; the core acceptance criteria still pass.
</summary>

```typescript
get merchantSuggestions(): string[] {
  const query = this.customMerchantInput.trim().toLowerCase();
  if (!query) {
    return [];
  }
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  const distinct: string[] = [];
  for (const m of this.allMerchants) {
    if (typeof m !== "string") continue;
    const trimmed = m.trim();
    if (!trimmed) continue;
    const lower = trimmed.toLowerCase();
    if (!seen.has(lower) && lower.includes(query)) {
      seen.add(lower);
      distinct.push(trimmed);
    }
  }

  distinct.sort((a, b) => {
    const aLower = a.toLowerCase();
    const bLower = b.toLowerCase();
    const aPrefix = aLower.startsWith(query);
    const bPrefix = bLower.startsWith(query);
    if (aPrefix !== bPrefix) {
      return aPrefix ? -1 : 1;
    }

    const aRecentIdx = this.recentTransactions.findIndex(
      (tx) =>
        tx && typeof tx.merchant === "string" && tx.merchant.trim().toLowerCase() === aLower,
    );
    const bRecentIdx = this.recentTransactions.findIndex(
      (tx) =>
        tx && typeof tx.merchant === "string" && tx.merchant.trim().toLowerCase() === bLower,
    );
    if (aRecentIdx !== -1 && bRecentIdx !== -1 && aRecentIdx !== bRecentIdx) {
      return aRecentIdx - bRecentIdx;
    }
    if (aRecentIdx !== -1 && bRecentIdx === -1) {
      return -1;
    }
    if (aRecentIdx === -1 && bRecentIdx !== -1) {
      return 1;
    }

    const aFreqIdx = this.frequentMerchants.findIndex(
      (m) => typeof m === "string" && m.trim().toLowerCase() === aLower,
    );
    const bFreqIdx = this.frequentMerchants.findIndex(
      (m) => typeof m === "string" && m.trim().toLowerCase() === bLower,
    );
    if (aFreqIdx !== -1 && bFreqIdx !== -1 && aFreqIdx !== bFreqIdx) {
      return aFreqIdx - bFreqIdx;
    }
    if (aFreqIdx !== -1 && bFreqIdx === -1) {
      return -1;
    }
    if (aFreqIdx === -1 && bFreqIdx !== -1) {
      return 1;
    }

    return a.localeCompare(b);
  });

  return distinct.slice(0, 5);
},
```

</details>

<details><summary><a href='https://github.com/fixture-owner/snaptally/pull/121/files#diff-42b67a3f58f6e0b4532f78652ecc5c7f6c9800f6ce1721f2b92cff4b51301f6aR513-R569'><strong>Possible Issue</strong></a>

`handleCustomMerchantKeydown` guards with `if (!this.merchantSuggestionsOpen || suggestions.length === 0)`, then inside that branch checks `event.key === "ArrowDown" && suggestions.length > 0`. When the dropdown is closed but suggestions exist, ArrowDown/ArrowUp open it and highlight — good. But when the dropdown is open and the user presses ArrowDown, `highlightedMerchantIndex` starts at -1 (set by `updateCustomMerchant`), so `(-1 + 1) % length = 0` — fine. However, `onCustomMerchantFocus` can set `merchantSuggestionsOpen = true` without resetting `highlightedMerchantIndex`, and `updateCustomMerchant` resets it to -1 on every keystroke, so after typing the highlight is always -1; pressing Enter with the dropdown open but no highlight does nothing (correct per tests). The concrete risk: `merchantSuggestions` is a getter recomputed on every access, and `handleCustomMerchantKeydown` reads it twice (`const suggestions = this.merchantSuggestions` then uses it), so index bounds are consistent — no bug there. Flagging only the focus-reopen path: after selecting a suggestion, `chooseMerchantSuggestion` calls `updateCustomMerchant` (which sets `merchantSuggestionsOpen = true`) then `closeMerchantSuggestions`. If the input retains focus, a subsequent `@focus` won't fire, so the dropdown stays closed until the user types — acceptable, but note the ordering dependency is fragile.
</summary>

```typescript
chooseMerchantSuggestion(suggestion: string): void {
  const trimmed = suggestion.trim();
  if (!trimmed) {
    return;
  }
  this.updateCustomMerchant(trimmed);
  this.closeMerchantSuggestions();
},

onCustomMerchantFocus(): void {
  if (this.customMerchantInput.trim().length > 0 && this.merchantSuggestions.length > 0) {
    this.merchantSuggestionsOpen = true;
  }
},

closeMerchantSuggestions(): void {
  this.merchantSuggestionsOpen = false;
  this.highlightedMerchantIndex = -1;
},

handleCustomMerchantKeydown(event: KeyboardEvent): void {
  const suggestions = this.merchantSuggestions;
  if (!this.merchantSuggestionsOpen || suggestions.length === 0) {
    if (event.key === "ArrowDown" && suggestions.length > 0) {
      this.merchantSuggestionsOpen = true;
      this.highlightedMerchantIndex = 0;
      event.preventDefault();
    } else if (event.key === "ArrowUp" && suggestions.length > 0) {
      this.merchantSuggestionsOpen = true;
      this.highlightedMerchantIndex = suggestions.length - 1;
      event.preventDefault();
    }
    return;
  }

  if (event.key === "ArrowDown") {
    event.preventDefault();
    this.highlightedMerchantIndex = (this.highlightedMerchantIndex + 1) % suggestions.length;
  } else if (event.key === "ArrowUp") {
    event.preventDefault();
    this.highlightedMerchantIndex =
      this.highlightedMerchantIndex <= 0
        ? suggestions.length - 1
        : this.highlightedMerchantIndex - 1;
  } else if (event.key === "Enter") {
    if (
      this.highlightedMerchantIndex >= 0 &&
      this.highlightedMerchantIndex < suggestions.length
    ) {
      event.preventDefault();
      this.chooseMerchantSuggestion(suggestions[this.highlightedMerchantIndex]);
    }
  } else if (event.key === "Escape") {
    event.preventDefault();
    this.closeMerchantSuggestions();
  }
},
```

</details>

<details><summary><a href='https://github.com/fixture-owner/snaptally/pull/121/files#diff-95d291e9ce4c8739cc7e65ff7bf0838dd5294cf39ab787ba51a42d08fb2df663R198-R223'><strong>Accessibility Gap</strong></a>

The combobox input declares `role="combobox"` and `aria-controls="merchant-suggestions-list"`, but the `<ul>` is rendered with `x-show` (display:none when hidden) rather than removed, and `aria-activedescendant` is bound to `undefined` when no highlight. More importantly, the input is missing `aria-haspopup="listbox"` and the listbox is not associated via `aria-owns`; some screen readers require the listbox to be a sibling referenced by `aria-controls` (it is) but the `role="option"` elements are `<li>` wrapping a `<button>`, which is invalid ARIA — an option should not contain an interactive button. This can cause screen readers to announce nested interactive controls and break option selection semantics. Use `<li role="option" @click=...>` directly instead of nesting a button.
</summary>

```txt
<ul
  id="merchant-suggestions-list"
  role="listbox"
  aria-label="Merchant suggestions"
  x-show="merchantSuggestionsOpen && merchantSuggestions.length > 0"
  x-transition
  class="absolute z-30 left-0 right-0 top-full mt-1 neu-raised-sm rounded-xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg border border-base-content/10 bg-[var(--neu-surface)] py-1 max-h-48 overflow-y-auto no-scrollbar"
>
  <template x-for="(s, index) in merchantSuggestions" :key="s">
    <li
      :id="`merchant-opt-${index}`"
      role="option"
      :aria-selected="highlightedMerchantIndex === index"
    >
      <button
        type="button"
        class="w-full text-left px-3 py-2 text-sm text-neu-text transition-colors flex items-center justify-between"
        :class="highlightedMerchantIndex === index ? 'neu-inset-sm font-semibold text-primary bg-primary/10' : 'hover:bg-base-content/5'"
        @mouseenter="highlightedMerchantIndex = index"
        @click="chooseMerchantSuggestion(s)"
      >
        <span x-text="s"></span>
      </button>
    </li>
  </template>
</ul>
```

</details>

</td></tr>
</table>

